          "Robert Cialdini's research on what he calls pre-suasion, laid out in his 2016 book of the same name, helps explain why the setup of a crucial conversation matters almost as much as the conversation itself. Cialdini found that the environment right before a high stakes exchange shapes how that exchange turns out more than most people realize. When a leader takes the time to create a calm, respectful setting before a hard conversation, establishing mutual purpose before the hard content even comes up, that leader is shaping the emotional and attentional context the hard content is about to land in, and that groundwork often matters more than the moment itself. Which means a leader who waits until a problem is urgent, letting the environment get charged before even attempting to talk about it, is walking into the worst possible conditions for the exact conversation they most need to have.",
